Z||000000||fierobob88||03-28-2001||11:57 PM||||Where can I find an exhaust for my 88 4 banger.  I have found the ocelet system in the fiero store.  But have also read about many people having flowmasters.  Anyone want to help me out?  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||03-29-2001||12:09 AM||||There are more single in/single out mufflers out there than we have time for   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The flowmaster that most on the forum talk about is the 40 & 80 dual out for the V6.<P>All the info you need again is in the SEARCH<P>Use muffler, flowmaster, headers, exhaust<BR>as the search word.<P>Jegs, Summit, JC Whitney, just a few catalogs you may want to check.<BR>Also there is a vendor list at the bottom of the page.<P><BR>DXR_DAD<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 03-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||03-29-2001||12:26 AM||||Hooker Tri-Y header. <P>I had one in my 87 with a Dynomax muffler and loved it. Keep the rest of the exhaust stock unless you want to flange the cat and switch to a straight pipe!<P>As your search will find there are some problems with the header and the starter but the thread search should point you in the right direction.||||reg||14
Z||000003||FieroJoe||03-29-2001||11:41 AM||||On the topic of this, for those who have gone with a flowmaster system, have you had to get custom bent pipes from the cat to the muffler?  I have tried all the autoparts places in my area and they can only get the pipe with the muffler attached.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Frank Sombers||03-31-2001||02:02 AM||||I just had (today) my stock '88 4-cylinder <BR>exhaust system replaced with a V6 system from<BR>a '86 SE. <BR>It cost me $25. for the muffler and tailpipes<BR>with excellent chrome tips at a salvage yard. <BR>I removed it myself with the help of a friend<BR>in about an hour. <BR>It was installed at a local muffler shop for $35.<BR>What did I get? A low cost system with original factory looks. Considering the  small performance difference you'll get in relation to the amount of money you'll spend on an after market system, I think going this<BR>route makes the most $en$e. <BR>||||reg||3
